# thoth — trust-on-first-use pinning of MCP tool definitions (0.42.0, T3): rug-pull defense.
#
# THE ATTACK. CVE-2025-54136 (CVSS 8.8) established that approving an MCP tool's definition does not
# survive later server-side changes: a server can advertise a benign `name`/`description`/`inputSchema`,
# be approved, and then silently swap the description for one carrying instructions to the model. The
# description is not inert data — thoth hands it to the model as tool metadata, and the model reads it as
# authoritative guidance about what the tool does and when to use it. That makes a changed description a
# prompt-injection vector with an approval already granted.
#
# thoth was fully exposed: `agent_format_tools`'s own comment calls the registry fields "semi-trusted",
# and thoth re-fetches them from daimon on every probe and passes them straight through. Nothing compared
# a tool's definition against what it looked like when the session started.
#
# ⚠ SCANNING CANNOT FIX THIS, and it is worth being clear why, because the instinct is to reach for the
# injection guard. A rug-pull is a change in something that was already accepted; the new description can
# be entirely innocuous-looking prose. The only control that actually works is IDENTITY: hash the
# definition, and notice when it stops matching. That is what this file does.
#
# THE POSTURE. Trust on first use, fail closed on change:
#   * First sight of a tool -> pin SHA-256 over `name \0 description \0 inputSchema`. Silent; a first
#     session has nothing to compare against and pretending otherwise would be theatre.
#   * Unchanged -> nothing said. Silence is the common case and must stay quiet.
#   * CHANGED -> the tool is WITHHELD from the advertisement AND refused at the dispatcher, and the
#     change is announced. Withholding keeps the swapped description out of the model's context, which
#     is the payload; refusing at dispatch is what makes "withheld" a control rather than a prediction.
#
#     ⚠ 0.44.4 CORRECTION. This line used to read "The model is never told the tool exists, so it
#     cannot call it." That was an inference about the model, not an enforcement: a name can arrive
#     from an earlier turn still in context, a hallucination, or the injected text this file defends
#     against — none of which consult the advertisement. The advertisement half shipped at 0.42.0
#     without the dispatch half, so for two minors a rug-pulled tool was invisible and fully
#     invokable. Both halves now exist; see `toolpin_withheld`.
#   * `/tools trust` accepts the new definitions and re-pins, for the legitimate case: a server upgrade.
#
# Withholding rather than warning-and-continuing is deliberate. A warning the operator scrolls past leaves
# the swapped description in the model's context, which is the entire payload. This is the same
# fail-closed direction as an absent t-ron falling to a confirm.
#
# ⚠ 0.42.0–0.50.1: SESSION-SCOPED. The pins lived in memory and died with the process, so this caught a swap
# DURING a session (or across `/reload` — never `/reprobe`, which re-probes hoosh, not the tools; the 0.42.0
# header claimed otherwise), not one BETWEEN two runs: a definition swapped while thoth was not running was
# accepted as first sight. 0.51.0 (F6, ADR-0022) closes that with a DURABLE STORE — the block below — and this
# paragraph says what the store can and cannot defend, in the same register as the rest of this header.
#
# THE STORE'S ATTACKER MODEL. thoth has no secret: a MAC key would live beside the store under the same uid,
# so a per-line HMAC is a per-line SHA-256 wearing a costume. The store therefore carries a whole-file
# SHA-256 trailer, and claims exactly what that buys. What it CLOSES: the CVE shape between runs — the MCP
# server or daimon swapping a definition while thoth is down; neither touches the file. What the trailer
# CATCHES: corruption (a torn write, a disk, an editor accident), a foreign writer, a stale format, a
# truncation — any of which REJECTS the whole file (a per-line skip would be "first sight" through the back
# door) and leaves it untouched as evidence. What it CANNOT catch and does not pretend to: a local user or a
# compromised process WITH THE OPERATOR'S UID editing, replacing or deleting the store (a deleted store is a
# silent first sight next run; the only signal is the greeting row reading `new` instead of `N pinned`). What
# does hold against that uid's neighbours: the file is created 0600 on every write (a temp file created
# exclusively at 0600, then renamed over the path — the inode is always thoth's own, so a pre-existing looser
# file is 0600 after the first flush, which neither the session nor the history store can say), a symlink at
# the path is refused before anything is read or written, and `[toolpin].file` is an AUTHORITY key (ADR-0021,
# global-only): a cloned repo cannot point thoth at a store of pre-approved pins it ships.
#
# Hashing is sigil's SHA-256 (already linked via the vendored bundle), NOT a cheap non-cryptographic hash.
# The attacker CHOOSES the replacement text, so a hash they can find a collision for is no control at all.

var TOOLPIN_MAX = 1024;            # pins in the table — live first sights AND a store's rows for this host (0.51.0: was 128; stored rows are only ever added, so they must not crowd out a live registry); past it the rest is advertised UNPINNED, and said
var TOOLPIN_NAME_CAP = 128;
var TOOLPIN_HASH_CAP = 72;         # 64 hex chars + NUL, rounded
var TOOLPIN_BLOB_CAP = 32768;      # the definition being hashed; a larger one is refused, not truncated

var _tp_n = 0;
var _tp_names = 0;                 # TOOLPIN_MAX * TOOLPIN_NAME_CAP
var _tp_hashes = 0;                # TOOLPIN_MAX * TOOLPIN_HASH_CAP
var _tp_blob = 0;                  # scratch for the canonical definition bytes
var _tp_changed = 0;               # tools withheld this probe because their definition moved
var _tp_withheld = 0;              # TOOLPIN_MAX bytes, parallel to _tp_names: 1 = this tool is withheld
var _tp_overflow = 0;              # 1 if the registry exceeded TOOLPIN_MAX
var _tp_pinned_at = 0;             # 0.51.0: TOOLPIN_MAX i64 slots (8 B each), parallel to _tp_names: epoch secs the pin was taken (0 = unknown)
var _tp_from_store = 0;            # 0.51.0: TOOLPIN_MAX bytes, parallel to _tp_names: 1 = the pin was loaded from the store (a previous run's)

fn toolpin_count() { return _tp_n; }
fn toolpin_changed() { return _tp_changed; }

# toolpin_withheld (0.44.4): 1 if `name` is a tool this session pinned and has since seen CHANGE.
#
# ⚠ WHY THIS EXISTS. Withholding a tool from the ADVERTISEMENT is not the same as blocking it, and
# this file used to claim it was: "The model is never told the tool exists, so it cannot call it."
# That is an inference about the model's behaviour, not a control — the model can name any tool it
# likes, from an earlier turn's context, a system prompt, a hallucination, or the very injection this
# defense exists for. Every LOCAL tool arm already enforces its own gate at dispatch rather than
# trusting the advertisement; the daimon arms did not, so a rug-pulled tool was invisible AND fully
# invokable. Read at the dispatcher by both executors (src/agent.cyr), before the t-ron gate.
#
# Returns 0 for a tool that was never pinned (including one advertised UNPINNED past TOOLPIN_MAX):
# a tool with no baseline cannot be said to have changed, and refusing it would be theatre.
fn toolpin_withheld(name) {
    if (config_toolpin_enabled() == 0) { return 0; }
    if (name == 0) { return 0; }
    if (_tp_withheld == 0) { return 0; }
    var idx = _tp_find(name);
    if (idx < 0) { return 0; }
    return load8(_tp_withheld + idx);
}
fn toolpin_reset() { _tp_n = 0; _tp_changed = 0; _tp_overflow = 0; return 0; }
# toolpin_pinned (0.51.0): 1 when `name` has a pin in the table (this session's or the store's).
fn toolpin_pinned(name) {
    if (name == 0) { return 0; }
    if (_tp_names == 0) { return 0; }
    if (_tp_find(name) >= 0) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}
# toolpin_store_greeting (0.51.0): the one-line note for the REPL banner (the TUI and the GUI carry the same facts
# as a row of the greeting box — greet_build). Sink-aware. Silent for the two quiet states (off, durable=false);
# every refusal is RED.
fn toolpin_store_greeting() {
    if (config_toolpin_enabled() == 0) { return 0; }
    var st = _tps_state;
    if (st == 8) { return 0; }
    emit("  ");
    if (st == 1) {
        emit(ui_sgr(ROLE_FAINT)); emit("tool pins: "); emit_clean_line(config_path_display(_tps_path)); emit(" ("); ofmt_int(_tps_loaded); emit(" pinned for this host");
        if (_tps_other > 0) { emit(", "); ofmt_int(_tps_other); emit(" other"); }
        emit(")"); emit(ui_reset()); oprintln("");
    } elif (st == 2) {
        emit(ui_sgr(ROLE_FAINT)); emit("tool pins: "); emit_clean_line(config_path_display(_tps_path)); emit(" (new — written at the first probe)"); emit(ui_reset()); oprintln("");
    } elif (st == 3) {
        ui_emit(ROLE_RED, "tool pins: "); emit_clean_line(config_path_display(_tps_path)); ui_emit(ROLE_RED, " REJECTED"); ui_emit(ROLE_FAINT, " — not a thoth pin store, or edited; session-scoped this run (/tools trust rewrites it)"); oprintln("");
    } elif (st == 4) {
        ui_emit(ROLE_RED, "tool pins: "); emit_clean_line(config_path_display(_tps_path)); ui_emit(ROLE_RED, " is a symlink"); ui_emit(ROLE_FAINT, " — refused; session-scoped this run"); oprintln("");
    } elif (st == 5) {
        ui_emit(ROLE_RED, "tool pins: cannot open "); emit_clean_line(config_path_display(_tps_path)); ui_emit(ROLE_FAINT, " — session-scoped this run"); oprintln("");
    } elif (st == 6) {
        ui_emit(ROLE_RED, "tool pins: "); emit_clean_line(config_path_display(_tps_path)); ui_emit(ROLE_RED, " larger than thoth can load"); ui_emit(ROLE_FAINT, " — session-scoped this run (nothing is written back)"); oprintln("");
    } elif (st == 9) {
        ui_emit(ROLE_RED, "tool pins: store write failed"); ui_emit(ROLE_FAINT, " — "); emit_clean_line(config_path_display(_tps_path)); ui_emit(ROLE_FAINT, "; pins are session-scoped from here"); oprintln("");
    } else {
        ui_emit(ROLE_FAINT, "tool pins: session-scoped — no ~/.thoth to keep them in (set [toolpin].file)"); oprintln("");
    }
    return 0;
}

fn _tp_bufs() {
    if (_tp_names == 0) { _tp_names = alloc(TOOLPIN_MAX * TOOLPIN_NAME_CAP); }
    if (_tp_hashes == 0) { _tp_hashes = alloc(TOOLPIN_MAX * TOOLPIN_HASH_CAP); }
    if (_tp_blob == 0) { _tp_blob = alloc(TOOLPIN_BLOB_CAP); }
    if (_tp_withheld == 0) { _tp_withheld = alloc(TOOLPIN_MAX); }
    if (_tp_pinned_at == 0) { _tp_pinned_at = alloc(TOOLPIN_MAX * 8); }
    if (_tp_from_store == 0) { _tp_from_store = alloc(TOOLPIN_MAX); }
    if (_tp_names == 0) { return 0; }
    if (_tp_hashes == 0) { return 0; }
    if (_tp_blob == 0) { return 0; }
    if (_tp_withheld == 0) { return 0; }
    if (_tp_pinned_at == 0) { return 0; }
    if (_tp_from_store == 0) { return 0; }
    return 1;
}
# _tp_add (0.51.0): append a pin row to the table — the one writer of _tp_names/_tp_hashes and their parallels
# (toolpin_check's first sight, and the store loader). Returns the index, or -1 when the table is full.
fn _tp_add(name, h, at, from_store) {
    if (_tp_n >= TOOLPIN_MAX) { return 0 - 1; }
    var nd = _tp_names + _tp_n * TOOLPIN_NAME_CAP;
    var hd = _tp_hashes + _tp_n * TOOLPIN_HASH_CAP;
    var i = 0;
    while ((load8(name + i) != 0) && (i < (TOOLPIN_NAME_CAP - 1))) { store8(nd + i, load8(name + i)); i = i + 1; }
    store8(nd + i, 0);
    var j = 0;
    while ((load8(h + j) != 0) && (j < (TOOLPIN_HASH_CAP - 1))) { store8(hd + j, load8(h + j)); j = j + 1; }
    store8(hd + j, 0);
    store8(_tp_withheld + _tp_n, 0);
    store64(_tp_pinned_at + _tp_n * 8, at);
    store8(_tp_from_store + _tp_n, from_store);
    _tp_n = _tp_n + 1;
    return _tp_n - 1;
}

# _tp_find: index of `name` in the pin table, or -1.
fn _tp_find(name) {
    var i = 0;
    while (i < _tp_n) {
        if (streq(_tp_names + i * TOOLPIN_NAME_CAP, name) == 1) { return i; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

# _tp_digest: SHA-256 hex over the tool's canonical definition, or 0 if it will not fit.
#
# NUL-separated rather than concatenated, so `{name:"ab", desc:"c"}` and `{name:"a", desc:"bc"}` cannot
# collide by construction — a separator an attacker cannot place inside a cstr field.
fn _tp_digest(name, desc, schema) {
    if (_tp_bufs() == 0) { return 0; }
    var off = 0;
    off = _append_cstr_cap(_tp_blob, off, TOOLPIN_BLOB_CAP, name);
    if (off >= (TOOLPIN_BLOB_CAP - 2)) { return 0; }
    store8(_tp_blob + off, 0); off = off + 1;
    if (desc != 0) { off = _append_cstr_cap(_tp_blob, off, TOOLPIN_BLOB_CAP, desc); }
    if (off >= (TOOLPIN_BLOB_CAP - 2)) { return 0; }
    store8(_tp_blob + off, 0); off = off + 1;
    if (schema != 0) { off = _append_cstr_cap(_tp_blob, off, TOOLPIN_BLOB_CAP, schema); }
    if (off >= (TOOLPIN_BLOB_CAP - 1)) { return 0; }
    return sha256_hex(_tp_blob, off);
}

# toolpin_check: the gate `agent_format_tools` calls per tool.
# Returns 1 to advertise the tool, 0 to WITHHOLD it.
#
# Order matters: a tool whose definition is too large to hash is advertised (returning 1), because
# refusing to advertise on a sizing limit would turn a capacity issue into a silent capability loss. The
# limit is 32 KB against a registry whose whole serialized form is capped at 32 KB, so it is a guard, not
# a live path.
fn toolpin_check(name, desc, schema) {
    if (config_toolpin_enabled() == 0) { return 1; }
    if (name == 0) { return 1; }
    if (load8(name) == 0) { return 1; }
    if (_tp_bufs() == 0) { return 1; }
    if (strlen(name) >= TOOLPIN_NAME_CAP) { return 1; }
    var h = _tp_digest(name, desc, schema);
    if (h == 0) { return 1; }                                # too large to hash -> do not lose the tool over it
    var idx = _tp_find(name);
    if (idx < 0) {
        # TRUST ON FIRST USE. Silent by design: a first sighting has nothing to compare against.
        if (_tp_n >= TOOLPIN_MAX) {
            if (_tp_overflow == 0) {
                _tp_overflow = 1;
                ui_emit(ROLE_FAINT, "  [toolpin] registry exceeds "); ofmt_int(TOOLPIN_MAX);
                ui_emit(ROLE_FAINT, " tools — the remainder are advertised UNPINNED"); oprintln("");
            }
            return 1;                                        # honest: unpinned, and said so
        }
        _tp_add(name, h, clock_epoch_secs(), 0);
        # 0.51.0: a new pin is a store change (flushed after the probe by the two probe sites); logged only when a
        # store is bound so the unbound path's log stays byte-identical to 0.50.x
        if (_tps_bound() == 1) {
            _tps_dirty = 1;
            log_begin("toolpin"); log_kv_str("tool", name); log_kv_str("result", "pinned_first_sight"); log_commit(SK_INFO);
        }
        return 1;
    }
    if (streq(_tp_hashes + idx * TOOLPIN_HASH_CAP, h) == 1) { store8(_tp_withheld + idx, 0); return 1; }   # unchanged — stay quiet, and lift any prior withhold

    # CHANGED. Withhold, and say so loudly: this is the CVE-2025-54136 shape.
    _tp_changed = _tp_changed + 1;
    store8(_tp_withheld + idx, 1);      # 0.44.4: also REFUSE it at the dispatcher — see toolpin_withheld
    # 0.44.2: the WITHHOLD is the whole point of this control — a rug-pulled tool vanishes from the
    # advertisement and the model is simply told it does not exist. If the reason is also invisible (as it
    # was under OUT_NULL: one-shot and the GUI), the operator sees an agent that has mysteriously stopped
    # using a tool and has nothing pointing at `/tools trust`. note_* keeps the reason on every surface.
    ui_emit(ROLE_RED, "  [toolpin] WITHHELD ");
    note_emit_label(name);                      # 0.45.2: the MCP server's own name for its tool — the label policy
    # 0.51.0: a pin from the STORE is a previous run's — the operator must be able to tell a between-runs swap
    # (the store's whole point) from an in-session one
    if (load8(_tp_from_store + idx) == 1) {
        var when = load64(_tp_pinned_at + idx * 8);
        if (when > 0) {
            var db[24];
            _tps_date_into(&db, when);
            note_emit(" — its MCP definition CHANGED since it was pinned on "); note_emit(&db); note_println(" (a previous run).");
        } else { note_println(" — its MCP definition CHANGED since a previous run pinned it."); }
    } else {
        note_println(" — its MCP definition CHANGED since this session pinned it.");
    }
    note_println("  a tool description is read by the model as instructions, so a silent swap is an injection with");
    note_println("  approval already granted. Not advertised this turn. `/tools trust` to accept and re-pin.");
    log_begin("toolpin"); log_kv_str("tool", name); log_kv_str("result", "withheld_definition_changed");
    if (load8(_tp_from_store + idx) == 1) { log_kv_int("pinned_at", load64(_tp_pinned_at + idx * 8)); }
    log_commit(SK_WARN);
    return 0;
}

# toolpin_trust_all: accept the current registry as the new baseline (`/tools trust`). Drops every pin so
# the next probe re-pins from scratch — the legitimate path after a server upgrade. 0.51.0: the pins of THIS
# host only (the table is host-scoped), the withheld flags cleared explicitly (they were left dirty, safe only
# by _tp_n bounding), and the store rewritten at once with this host's rows gone (other hosts' kept) — an
# authority act with no gate (a typed slash command is the operator's own hand), so it is logged at WARN.
var _tps_trust_wrote = 0;                  # 0.51.0: 1 when the last `/tools trust` rewrote the store (the command says which)
fn toolpin_trust_wrote() { return _tps_trust_wrote; }
fn toolpin_trust_all() {
    var had = _tp_n;
    _tp_n = 0;
    _tp_changed = 0;
    _tp_overflow = 0;
    if (_tp_withheld != 0) { var i = 0; while (i < TOOLPIN_MAX) { store8(_tp_withheld + i, 0); i = i + 1; } }
    log_begin("toolpin"); log_kv_str("result", "trusted"); log_kv_str("host", _tps_host_cstr()); log_kv_int("dropped", had); log_commit(SK_WARN);
    _tps_trust_wrote = 0;
    if (_tps_path != 0) {
        var before = _tps_written;
        _tps_dirty = 1;
        _tps_trusting = 1;                       # this flush drops this host's FILE rows too (operator intent)
        toolpin_store_flush();
        _tps_trusting = 0;
        if (_tps_written > before) { _tps_trust_wrote = 1; }
    }
    return had;
}

# --- the durable store (0.51.0, F6; ADR-0022) ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# FORMAT — line-oriented text, greppable evidence after an incident, one whole-file digest:
#     THOTH-TOOLPIN-1\n
#     <host>\t<name>\t<sha256 hex, 64>\t<pinned epoch secs>\n          (0..TPS_ROWS_MAX rows)
#     DIGEST\t<sha256 hex over every byte before this line>\n
# `host` is `[daimon].url` at pin time (already label-cleaned at config load: no C0 byte, so no tab or newline)
# — a pin is HOST + NAME, because that is the identity daimon exposes (no server id on the wire): the same name
# on another daimon is another tool, never "CHANGED". A name holding a byte < 0x20 or DEL is not storable
# (pinned in memory only, counted). The digest makes per-row escaping unnecessary: a row that does not parse
# is a rejected FILE, never a skipped line.
#
# LIFECYCLE — bound ONCE in main.cyr for every front end (REPL, TUI, GUI, one-shot — an unattended run is
# where a between-runs swap pays most); the table holds the bound host's rows (`_tp_from_store` = 1); the two
# probe sites flush after `agent_format_tools` when a first sight made the table dirty; `/tools trust`
# flushes at once; `/reload` rebinds the host namespace when `[daimon].url` moved. A flush RE-READS and
# re-verifies the file, keeps other hosts' rows verbatim, UNIONS same-host rows another thoth pinned since
# this one loaded (a pin is never downgraded — except under `/tools trust`, when this host's file rows go),
# then writes by temp + exclusive create + rename. Two sessions trusting the same host at once are
# last-writer-wins on that host's rows (the history file's residual, named).
#
# STATES (toolpin_store_state): 0 unbound · 1 loaded · 2 new (no file yet; the first probe creates it) ·
# 3 REJECTED (magic / row / digest — the file is left as evidence; only `/tools trust` rewrites it) ·
# 4 a symlink at the path (nothing read, nothing ever written) · 5 unreadable · 6 larger than the load cap
# (never written back, not even by `/tools trust` — a file thoth did not read whole is never rewritten from a
# partial picture, the 0.48.0 rule) · 7 no path (no ~/.thoth, and [toolpin].file unset) · 8 [toolpin].durable =
# false · 9 a write FAILED mid-session (persistence stopped; the path is kept so the surfaces can name it, and
# `/tools trust` may try the rewrite again). Every state ≥ 3 is announced on every surface (a defence that
# silently fell back to session-scoped would be the exact silence this file exists to break); pins stay
# session-scoped for the run. `_tps_broke` is the once-notice latch the REPL/TUI ack; state 9 is the fact.
var TPS_MAGIC = "THOTH-TOOLPIN-1";
var TPS_ROWS_MAX = 8192;           # rows a file may carry (every host) — a `var` so a test can lower it; the WRITER refuses to exceed it (never a file thoth then rejects as edited)
var TPS_READ_CAP = 1048576;        # 1 MiB — a `var` so a test can lower it; the buffers are alloc'd at first use
var TPS_SECS_MAX = 253402300799;   # 9999-12-31: a larger epoch is a crafted row (and would spin the date walk)
var TPS_HOST_CAP = 512;
var TPS_MODE = 0x180;              # 0600 — the CREATE mode of every temp the store is renamed from
var _tps_path = 0;                 # the bound path (0 = session-scoped)
var _tps_state = 0;
var _tps_loaded = 0;               # rows loaded for the bound host
var _tps_other = 0;                # rows of other hosts in the file (carried, never loaded)
var _tps_broke = 0;                # 1: a write failed mid-session (persistence stopped; announce once)
var _tps_dirty = 0;                # pins added / trusted since the last flush
var _tps_trusting = 0;             # 1 during a `/tools trust` flush: this host's file rows are dropped
var _tps_host = 0;                 # TPS_HOST_CAP bytes: the host the table is keyed to
var _tps_buf = 0;                  # TPS_READ_CAP: the file as read
var _tps_out = 0;                  # TPS_READ_CAP: the rewrite image
var _tps_unstorable = 0;           # pins of this host whose name the store cannot carry
var _tps_written = 0;              # flushes that wrote (a /state fact)
fn toolpin_store_state() { return _tps_state; }
fn toolpin_store_path() { return _tps_path; }
fn toolpin_store_loaded() { return _tps_loaded; }
fn toolpin_store_other() { return _tps_other; }
fn toolpin_store_broke() { return _tps_broke; }
fn toolpin_store_broke_ack() { _tps_broke = 0; return 0; }
fn toolpin_store_unstorable() { return _tps_unstorable; }
fn toolpin_store_dirty() { return _tps_dirty; }
fn toolpin_store_written() { return _tps_written; }
fn toolpin_store_host() { return _tps_host_cstr(); }
fn _tps_bound() { if (_tps_path == 0) { return 0; } if ((_tps_state == 1) || (_tps_state == 2)) { return 1; } return 0; }
fn _tps_bufs() {
    if (_tps_host == 0) { _tps_host = alloc(TPS_HOST_CAP); if (_tps_host != 0) { store8(_tps_host, 0); } }
    if (_tps_buf == 0) { _tps_buf = alloc(TPS_READ_CAP); }
    if (_tps_out == 0) { _tps_out = alloc(TPS_READ_CAP); }
    if ((_tps_host == 0) || (_tps_buf == 0) || (_tps_out == 0)) { return 0; }
    return 1;
}
fn _tps_host_cstr() { if (_tps_host == 0) { return ""; } return _tps_host; }
# _tps_host_canon: `[daimon].url` (label-cleaned at load; "" when unset) as a pin-namespace key — ASCII letters
# lower-cased and trailing slashes dropped, clipped at TPS_HOST_CAP-1, so a re-spelling of the SAME daimon
# (`HTTP://Host:8090/` vs `http://host:8090`) is the same namespace, not a fresh first sight. `[daimon].url` is a
# layered preference a repo may set; a repo pointing thoth at ANOTHER daimon is, honestly, another namespace —
# its tools are that daimon's — and the greeting / `/state` say so when the local layer set the url.
fn _tps_host_canon(dst, u) {
    var i = 0;
    if (u != 0) {
        while ((load8(u + i) != 0) && (i < (TPS_HOST_CAP - 1))) {
            var b = load8(u + i) & 255;
            if ((b >= 65) && (b <= 90)) { b = b + 32; }
            store8(dst + i, b);
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }
    while ((i > 0) && (load8(dst + i - 1) == 47)) { i = i - 1; }
    store8(dst + i, 0);
    return i;
}
fn _tps_host_set() {
    if (_tps_bufs() == 0) { return 0; }
    _tps_host_canon(_tps_host, config_daimon_url());
    return 0;
}
fn _tps_host_moved() {                      # 1 when config's daimon url (canonical) is not the table's host
    var cb[520];
    _tps_host_canon(&cb, config_daimon_url());
    if (streq(_tps_host_cstr(), &cb) == 1) { return 0; }
    return 1;
}
# _tps_name_storable: 1 when every byte of the name can ride a row (no C0, no DEL — the tab and newline the
# format hangs on are C0 bytes).
fn _tps_name_storable(name) {
    var i = 0;
    while (load8(name + i) != 0) {
        var b = load8(name + i) & 255;
        if (b < 32) { return 0; }
        if (b == 127) { return 0; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if (i == 0) { return 0; }
    return 1;
}
fn _tps_hex_ok(p, n) {                      # exactly n lowercase hex digits at p
    var i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        var b = load8(p + i) & 255;
        var ok = 0;
        if ((b >= 48) && (b <= 57)) { ok = 1; }
        if ((b >= 97) && (b <= 102)) { ok = 1; }
        if (ok == 0) { return 0; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 1;
}
fn _tps_date_into(dst, epoch) {             # "YYYY-MM-DD" (the first ten bytes of the ISO form) + NUL
    var iso = iso8601(epoch);
    var i = 0;
    while ((i < 10) && (load8(iso + i) != 0)) { store8(dst + i, load8(iso + i)); i = i + 1; }
    store8(dst + i, 0);
    return 0;
}
# _tps_line_end: index of the '\n' ending the line that starts at `i`, or -1 when none before `len`.
fn _tps_line_end(buf, i, len) {
    while (i < len) { if (load8(buf + i) == 10) { return i; } i = i + 1; }
    return 0 - 1;
}
# _tps_field: the end (exclusive) of the field starting at `i` and ending at '\t' before `end`, or -1.
fn _tps_field(buf, i, end) {
    while (i < end) { if (load8(buf + i) == 9) { return i; } i = i + 1; }
    return 0 - 1;
}
# _tps_parse: verify the file image — the magic, every row's shape, the row count, the digest — and, when `apply`
# is 1, load the bound host's rows into the table (others counted). Returns 1 when the WHOLE file verifies. ⚠ With
# `apply` = 1 rows are added AS THEY VERIFY, so a failure past a good row would leave rows behind — every caller
# verifies with `apply` = 0 FIRST and applies only a file that passed (the review's finding: the bind path used
# to apply in one pass, and a file rejected on its 1025th row had loaded its first rows as a previous run's pins).
# Pure over `buf`.
var _tps_p_loaded = 0;
var _tps_p_other = 0;
var _tps_p_rows = 0;
var _tps_p_digest_at = 0;                   # offset of the DIGEST line (the bytes before it are the digested image)
var _tps_p_rows_at = 0;                     # offset of the first row
fn _tps_parse(buf, len, apply) {
    _tps_p_loaded = 0; _tps_p_other = 0; _tps_p_rows = 0; _tps_p_digest_at = 0 - 1; _tps_p_rows_at = 0;
    if (_tp_bufs() == 0) { return 0; }
    var ml = strlen(TPS_MAGIC);
    if (len < (ml + 1)) { return 0; }
    if (memeq(buf, TPS_MAGIC, ml) == 0) { return 0; }
    if (load8(buf + ml) != 10) { return 0; }
    # pass 1: find the digest line (the LAST line) and verify it against everything before it
    if (load8(buf + len - 1) != 10) { return 0; }
    var dl = len - 1;
    while ((dl > 0) && (load8(buf + dl - 1) != 10)) { dl = dl - 1; }
    if ((len - dl) != (7 + 64 + 1)) { return 0; }
    if (memeq(buf + dl, "DIGEST\t", 7) == 0) { return 0; }
    if (_tps_hex_ok(buf + dl + 7, 64) == 0) { return 0; }
    var want = sha256_hex(buf, dl);
    if (want == 0) { return 0; }
    if (memeq(want, buf + dl + 7, 64) == 0) { return 0; }
    _tps_p_digest_at = dl;
    _tps_p_rows_at = ml + 1;
    # pass 2: the rows — exactly four fields; host 1..TPS_HOST_CAP-1 bytes, name 1..TOOLPIN_NAME_CAP-1 bytes,
    # both without C0/DEL; the hash 64 hex; the secs up to 19 digits
    var i = ml + 1;
    var host = _tps_host_cstr();
    var hl = strlen(host);
    var nb[128];
    var hb[72];
    while (i < dl) {
        var e = _tps_line_end(buf, i, dl);
        if (e < 0) { return 0; }
        _tps_p_rows = _tps_p_rows + 1;
        if (_tps_p_rows > TPS_ROWS_MAX) { return 0; }
        var f1 = _tps_field(buf, i, e);          if (f1 < 0) { return 0; }
        var f2 = _tps_field(buf, f1 + 1, e);     if (f2 < 0) { return 0; }
        var f3 = _tps_field(buf, f2 + 1, e);     if (f3 < 0) { return 0; }
        if (_tps_field(buf, f3 + 1, e) >= 0) { return 0; }                    # a fifth field
        var hlen = f1 - i; var nlen = f2 - f1 - 1; var xlen = f3 - f2 - 1; var slen = e - f3 - 1;
        if ((hlen < 1) || (hlen >= TPS_HOST_CAP)) { return 0; }
        if ((nlen < 1) || (nlen >= TOOLPIN_NAME_CAP)) { return 0; }
        if (xlen != 64) { return 0; }
        if ((slen < 1) || (slen > 19)) { return 0; }
        var k = 0;
        while (k < hlen) { var b = load8(buf + i + k) & 255; if ((b < 32) || (b == 127)) { return 0; } k = k + 1; }
        k = 0;
        while (k < nlen) { var c = load8(buf + f1 + 1 + k) & 255; if ((c < 32) || (c == 127)) { return 0; } k = k + 1; }
        if (_tps_hex_ok(buf + f2 + 1, 64) == 0) { return 0; }
        var secs = 0;
        k = 0;
        while (k < slen) { var d = load8(buf + f3 + 1 + k) & 255; if ((d < 48) || (d > 57)) { return 0; } secs = secs * 10 + (d - 48); k = k + 1; }
        if ((secs < 0) || (secs > TPS_SECS_MAX)) { return 0; }
        var mine = 0;
        if (hlen == hl) { if (memeq(buf + i, host, hl) == 1) { mine = 1; } }
        if (mine == 1) {
            _tps_p_loaded = _tps_p_loaded + 1;
            if (apply == 1) {
                memcpy(&nb, buf + f1 + 1, nlen); store8(&nb + nlen, 0);
                memcpy(&hb, buf + f2 + 1, 64); store8(&hb + 64, 0);
                if (_tp_find(&nb) < 0) { if (_tp_add(&nb, &hb, secs, 1) < 0) { _tp_overflow = 1; } }
            }
        } else { _tps_p_other = _tps_p_other + 1; }
        i = e + 1;
    }
    return 1;
}
# _tps_exists: 1 when something is at `path` — by stat, not by open, so a file the operator cannot READ (mode
# 000, another owner) still counts as present (`file_exists` is an open(O_RDONLY) and reads such a file as absent —
# the review's finding: an unreadable store bound as `new` and the first flush renamed over it).
fn _tps_exists(path) {
    var st[256];
    if (xstat(path, &st) == 0) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}
# _tps_read: the file into _tps_buf. Returns the byte count, -1 absent/unreadable, -2 at/over the cap
# (never partially loaded — the 0.48.0 session-store rule: a file thoth did not read whole is never written back).
fn _tps_read(path) {
    if (_tps_bufs() == 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    var fd = file_open(path, O_RDONLY, 0);
    if (fd < 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    var total = 0;
    var go = 1;
    while (go == 1) {
        var got = file_read(fd, _tps_buf + total, TPS_READ_CAP - total);
        if (got <= 0) { go = 0; }
        else { total = total + got; if (total >= TPS_READ_CAP) { go = 0; } }
    }
    var over = 0;
    if (total >= TPS_READ_CAP) { over = 1; }
    file_close(fd);
    if (over == 1) { return 0 - 2; }
    return total;
}
# _tps_load: bind-time load — sets _tps_state and fills the table for the bound host. Never prints.
fn _tps_load(path) {
    _tps_loaded = 0; _tps_other = 0;
    if (is_symlink(str_from(path)) == 1) { _tps_state = 4; return 0; }
    var n = _tps_read(path);
    if (n == (0 - 2)) { _tps_state = 6; return 0; }
    if (n < 0) {
        if (_tps_exists(path) == 1) { _tps_state = 5; return 0; }
        _tps_state = 2;                          # no file yet: the first probe creates it — the ONLY road to first sight
        return 0;
    }
    if (_tps_parse(_tps_buf, n, 0) == 0) { _tps_state = 3; return 0; }   # verify the WHOLE file first —
    _tps_parse(_tps_buf, n, 1);                                          # — then apply (nothing of a rejected file lands)
    _tps_loaded = _tps_p_loaded; _tps_other = _tps_p_other;
    _tps_state = 1;
    return 0;
}
# toolpin_store_init: bind the store at `path` (0 = none). ONE call, main.cyr, for every front end. Never prints —
# the surfaces read the getters (the greeting row, /state, the window's notices, one-shot's stderr).
fn toolpin_store_init(path) {
    _tps_path = 0; _tps_state = 0; _tps_loaded = 0; _tps_other = 0; _tps_broke = 0; _tps_dirty = 0; _tps_unstorable = 0; _tps_written = 0;
    if (_tp_bufs() == 0) { return 0; }
    _tps_host_set();
    if (path == 0) {
        if (config_toolpin_durable() == 0) { _tps_state = 8; } else { _tps_state = 7; }
        return 0;
    }
    _tps_path = path;
    _tps_load(path);
    log_begin("toolpin_store"); log_kv_str("path", path); log_kv_str("host", _tps_host_cstr()); log_kv_int("state", _tps_state);
    log_kv_int("pins", _tps_loaded); log_kv_int("other", _tps_other);
    if (_tps_state <= 2) { log_commit(SK_INFO); } else { log_commit(SK_ERROR); }
    return 0;
}
# _tps_append: bytes into the rewrite image (bounded; -1 when they do not fit).
fn _tps_append(off, p, n) {
    if (off < 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    if ((off + n) >= TPS_READ_CAP) { return 0 - 1; }
    memcpy(_tps_out + off, p, n);
    return off + n;
}
fn _tps_append_cstr(off, s) { return _tps_append(off, s, strlen(s)); }
# _tps_render: the rewrite image — the header, every OTHER host's row from the fresh read (verbatim; `have` = 1
# when a verified image sits in _tps_buf), this host's rows from the table (storable names only), the digest.
# Returns the image length, or -1 when it does not fit.
var _tps_render_rows = 0;                  # rows the last render put in the image (the writer's own count against TPS_ROWS_MAX)
fn _tps_render(have, blen) {
    var off = _tps_append_cstr(0, TPS_MAGIC);
    off = _tps_append(off, "\n", 1);
    _tps_render_rows = 0;
    if (have == 1) {
        var i = _tps_p_rows_at;
        var host = _tps_host_cstr();
        var hl = strlen(host);
        while ((i < _tps_p_digest_at) && (off >= 0)) {
            var e = _tps_line_end(_tps_buf, i, _tps_p_digest_at);
            if (e < 0) { i = _tps_p_digest_at; }
            else {
                var f1 = _tps_field(_tps_buf, i, e);
                var mine = 0;
                if ((f1 - i) == hl) { if (memeq(_tps_buf + i, host, hl) == 1) { mine = 1; } }
                if (mine == 0) { off = _tps_append(off, _tps_buf + i, e + 1 - i); _tps_render_rows = _tps_render_rows + 1; }
                i = e + 1;
            }
        }
    }
    _tps_unstorable = 0;
    var k = 0;
    var sb[24];
    var hstor = 1;
    if (load8(_tps_host_cstr()) == 0) { hstor = 0; }   # an empty host cannot ride a row (the parser wants 1+ bytes): its pins stay in memory
    while ((k < _tp_n) && (off >= 0)) {
        var nm = _tp_names + k * TOOLPIN_NAME_CAP;
        if ((_tps_name_storable(nm) == 0) || (hstor == 0)) { _tps_unstorable = _tps_unstorable + 1; }
        else {
            _tps_render_rows = _tps_render_rows + 1;
            if (_tps_render_rows > TPS_ROWS_MAX) { return 0 - 1; }   # the writer stops where the reader would reject — never a file thoth then calls edited
            off = _tps_append_cstr(off, _tps_host_cstr());
            off = _tps_append(off, "\t", 1);
            off = _tps_append_cstr(off, nm);
            off = _tps_append(off, "\t", 1);
            off = _tps_append_cstr(off, _tp_hashes + k * TOOLPIN_HASH_CAP);
            off = _tps_append(off, "\t", 1);
            var at = load64(_tp_pinned_at + k * 8);
            if (at < 0) { at = 0; }
            var sl = fmt_int_buf(at, &sb);
            off = _tps_append(off, &sb, sl);
            off = _tps_append(off, "\n", 1);
        }
        k = k + 1;
    }
    if (off < 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    var dg = sha256_hex(_tps_out, off);
    if (dg == 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    off = _tps_append_cstr(off, "DIGEST\t");
    off = _tps_append(off, dg, 64);
    off = _tps_append(off, "\n", 1);
    return off;
}
# _tps_write_atomic: `<path>.tmp.<pid>.<16 hex>` created EXCLUSIVELY at 0600 (the hook capture's unguessable
# name; up to 8 tries), the image written whole, fsync'd, closed, then renamed over the path — the inode is
# always thoth's own, so the mode is enforced on every write; a symlink planted at the path after the bind is
# REPLACED by the rename, never followed. Returns 0, or -1 with the temp unlinked. Portability, named: AGNOS
# drops O_EXCL (a plain create) and fsyncs the whole fs; Windows's rename is MoveFileEx(REPLACE_EXISTING).
fn _tps_write_atomic(path, buf, len) {
    var pl = strlen(path);
    var tmp = alloc(pl + 48);
    if (tmp == 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    var tries = 0;
    var fd = 0 - 1;
    while ((tries < 8) && (fd < 0)) {
        var off = 0;
        memcpy(tmp, path, pl); off = pl;
        memcpy(tmp + off, ".tmp.", 5); off = off + 5;
        var ib[24];
        var il = fmt_int_buf(getpid(), &ib);
        memcpy(tmp + off, &ib, il); off = off + il;
        store8(tmp + off, 46); off = off + 1;
        var rb[8];
        store64(&rb, 0);
        random_bytes(&rb, 8);
        var k = 0;
        while (k < 8) {
            var b = load8(&rb + k) & 255;
            store8(tmp + off, load8("0123456789abcdef" + (b >> 4))); off = off + 1;
            store8(tmp + off, load8("0123456789abcdef" + (b & 15))); off = off + 1;
            k = k + 1;
        }
        store8(tmp + off, 0);
        fd = file_open(tmp, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_EXCL, TPS_MODE);
        tries = tries + 1;
    }
    if (fd < 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    var w = 0;
    var ok = 1;
    while ((w < len) && (ok == 1)) {
        var n = file_write(fd, buf + w, len - w);
        if (n <= 0) { ok = 0; } else { w = w + n; }
    }
    if (ok == 1) { if (xfsync(fd) < 0) { ok = 0; } }
    file_close(fd);
    if (ok == 1) { if (file_rename(tmp, path) < 0) { ok = 0; } }
    if (ok == 0) { xunlink(tmp); return 0 - 1; }
    return 0;
}
# toolpin_store_flush: write the store when the table is dirty. Re-reads and re-verifies the file first — other
# hosts' rows ride verbatim, same-host rows another session pinned since this one loaded are UNIONED into the
# table (never downgraded; dropped only under `/tools trust`), a file that turned bad underneath (edited,
# replaced by a symlink, grown past the cap, or one the writer cannot fit within its own row cap) is NOT written
# over — persistence stops (state 9 + the broke latch) and the surfaces say so; the pins still guard the session.
# A rejected-at-bind store (3), an unreadable one (5) or a failed writer (9) is rewritten FRESH only under
# `/tools trust`; an over-cap one (6) never (a file thoth did not read whole is never rewritten from a partial
# picture); a symlink (4) never. Returns 0, or -1 when persistence stopped this call.
fn _tps_fail(why) {
    _tps_state = 9;
    _tps_broke = 1;
    log_begin("toolpin_store"); log_kv_str("result", "write_failed"); log_kv_str("why", why); log_kv_str("host", _tps_host_cstr()); log_commit(SK_ERROR);
    return 0 - 1;
}
fn toolpin_store_flush() {
    if (_tps_path == 0) { return 0; }
    if (_tps_dirty == 0) { return 0; }
    if ((_tps_state == 4) || (_tps_state == 6)) { return 0; }                # never written: a symlink, a file not read whole
    if ((_tps_state == 3) || (_tps_state == 5) || (_tps_state == 9)) {
        if (_tps_trusting == 0) { return 0; }                                 # session-scoped this run
        if (is_symlink(str_from(_tps_path)) == 1) { _tps_state = 4; return 0; }
        var fl = _tps_render(0, 0);
        if (fl < 0) { return _tps_fail("render"); }
        if (_tps_write_atomic(_tps_path, _tps_out, fl) < 0) { return _tps_fail("write"); }
        _tps_dirty = 0; _tps_written = _tps_written + 1; _tps_state = 1; _tps_loaded = _tp_n; _tps_other = 0;
        log_begin("toolpin_store"); log_kv_str("result", "rewritten_after_reject"); log_kv_str("host", _tps_host_cstr()); log_kv_int("pins", _tp_n); log_commit(SK_WARN);
        return 0;
    }
    if (is_symlink(str_from(_tps_path)) == 1) { _tps_state = 4; _tps_broke = 1; return 0 - 1; }   # a link planted after the bind: refused, never written through
    var have = 0;
    var n = _tps_read(_tps_path);
    if (n == (0 - 2)) { _tps_state = 6; _tps_broke = 1; return 0 - 1; }   # grown past the cap underneath: never written back
    if (n >= 0) {
        if (_tps_parse(_tps_buf, n, 0) == 0) { _tps_state = 3; _tps_broke = 1; return 0 - 1; }   # edited underneath: left as evidence
        have = 1;
        if (_tps_trusting == 0) { _tps_parse(_tps_buf, n, 1); }              # union: another session's same-host pins
        _tps_other = _tps_p_other;
    } elif (_tps_exists(_tps_path) == 1) { return _tps_fail("unreadable"); }   # present but unreadable now
    var fl2 = _tps_render(have, n);
    if (fl2 < 0) { return _tps_fail("full"); }                                # the writer's own row / byte cap — never a file the reader would reject
    if (_tps_write_atomic(_tps_path, _tps_out, fl2) < 0) { return _tps_fail("write"); }
    _tps_dirty = 0; _tps_written = _tps_written + 1; _tps_state = 1;
    _tps_loaded = 0;
    var k = 0;
    while (k < _tp_n) { if (_tps_name_storable(_tp_names + k * TOOLPIN_NAME_CAP) == 1) { _tps_loaded = _tps_loaded + 1; } k = k + 1; }
    if (load8(_tps_host_cstr()) == 0) { _tps_loaded = 0; }
    log_begin("toolpin_store"); log_kv_str("result", "written"); log_kv_str("host", _tps_host_cstr()); log_kv_int("pins", _tps_loaded); log_kv_int("other", _tps_other); log_commit(SK_INFO);
    return 0;
}
# toolpin_host_rebind: `/reload` moved `[daimon].url` — flush this host's pins, clear the table (the withheld
# flags with it: a different host is a different namespace, never "CHANGED"), load the new host's rows.
fn toolpin_host_rebind() {
    if (_tps_host_moved() == 0) { return 0; }
    toolpin_store_flush();
    _tp_n = 0; _tp_changed = 0; _tp_overflow = 0;
    if (_tp_withheld != 0) { var i = 0; while (i < TOOLPIN_MAX) { store8(_tp_withheld + i, 0); i = i + 1; } }
    _tps_host_set();
    _tps_dirty = 0;
    if (_tps_path != 0) { _tps_load(_tps_path); }
    log_begin("toolpin_store"); log_kv_str("result", "host_rebind"); log_kv_str("host", _tps_host_cstr()); log_kv_int("state", _tps_state); log_kv_int("pins", _tps_loaded); log_commit(SK_INFO);
    return 0;
}
